> One of the Valks I assended wielded
> Magicbane (and nothing else---the only reflection I'd found was
> a shield) during her ascension run.

This looks like it could be a conduct game, otherwise why not wish for 
reflection, and MR if you were relying on Magicbane for that? There's 
also polypiling and reverse-genociding dragons. If it was a conduct 
game, or maybe you blew up your BoH (I know what that feels like!), 
obviously priorities change in these circumstances.

> It's certainly not the best
> with regards to damage, but it's usually sufficient

Sure it's *usually* sufficient.

> and none of
> the other alternatives protect against cursing.

True, but I've only ever found cursing to be a mild annoyance at worst. 
The only exception I'm aware of is when wielding a two-handed weapon*. 
In other cases, I'm not going to give up several points of damage from 
each hit just to prevent a handful of curses over the course of a game.

*Possibly also a cursed weapon *and* shield; but as long as you keep 
both blessed, this is really very unlikely.



On reconsidering, I would say the following:

I agree that it makes sense to enchant Magicbane to +6/7, if you decide 
to use it for killing stuff.

All Magicbane's other effects are pretty much irrelevant for deciding 
what to hit things with on a regular, non-conduct ascension run.

For wizards, wielding Magicbane makes sense unless something better 
drops in your lap as it's the first sacrifice gift and the whole thing 
is marginal for them anyway.

You could also make a case for some other classes if there are too many 
artifacts to wish for another and sacrificing for a better weapon is 
looking like a hard road. Personally I try to get to the Castle wand and 
wish for something better rather than get into this situation, although 
this is not always possible.


